A||||6||Eric||Double Clutching||||1||
Z||000000||Eric||06-08-2001||10:06 PM||||I was watching the movie Grand Prix a couple of weeks ago, and I thought it was so cool how all those drivers could double clutch so well.  They had a shot of the driver's feet pushing the clutch pedal twice for every downshift.  And the sound...  Well, needless to say, I had to give it a shot in my Fiero.  It was difficult at first, but now I've got the hang of it pretty well.  Should I worry about hurting my clutch or transmission if I continue doing this?  Why do the old time race cars need to do this type of downshift while today they can just heel-toe?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Monkeyman||06-08-2001||10:13 PM||||The double clutching stopped with the invention of synchronization.  If you want some practice double clutching, try driving a semi.  Of course, once you get used to the big truck you'd learn to float the gears.  I'd be afraid to try that with a automobile tranny.  I'd think it wouldn't be heavy duty enough.<P>MM||||reg||1
Z||000002||grizco||06-08-2001||10:25 PM||||If you want to double clutch the semi you need to drive is the 1960 Mack that my dad has on his farm. There no synchronization in the transmission and you have three sticks for shifting between the 15 gears. <P>josh<P>P.S. I learned to drive that truck before I had my license to drive a car.||||reg||1
Z||000003||ChadMan||06-08-2001||10:45 PM||||Eric, if you start double-clutching all the time, seems like your throwout bearing would wear out twice as fast, but your gearbox synchros would last forever!||||reg||10
Z||000004||Hank is Here||06-09-2001||01:41 AM||||Well double clutching is still alive and well.  Even with the advent of synchros and all that, large trucks still need to be double cluthced. It seems to me the Road-Ranger trannies are the worst for this, but this is probably my in-expierence. At least when I drive anything over about 30,000 GVW or so I need to either power shift (no clutch) or double clutch, if you try to shift normally you will grind the gears.   <P>And yes the old tri-plexe and quadra-plexe Macks take the cake for worst shifting---in my book at least||||reg||1
Z||000005||danny boy||06-09-2001||04:11 AM|||| Double clutching is only necessary in a big truck. Unless of course you have one of those goofy auto shift things.Just use the clutch for start and stop.My Aunt/Uncles Volvo 660 had one. I hated it. Kept trying to reach for the shifter.<BR> How can you tell a driver fresh out of training? He's still wearing his company hat and its clean. And "Good Buddy" means you're gay.||||reg||1
Z||000006||MrPBody||06-09-2001||10:56 AM||||Old F*rt speaks:<P>With any modern transmission -- I'm talkin' cars, not big trucks -- double-clutching on upshift is not necessary.  (But if you feel lke doing it, don't let me stop you.)  However, double-clutching on downshift <I>can</I> help you shift quickly and smoothly into a lower gear.<P>I find that Fiero's pedals are well arranged for "heel-and-toe" double-clutching.  I don't literally use the heel and toe of my right foot; more like "big toe - little toe" or "left edge -- right edge".<BR>||||reg||1
